\f1\fs26 Sarah took a seat at the bar. Johnny regarded the one adjacent as if it was some bacteria-incubating, medieval birthing stool. Reluctantly, he pecked a few napkins from a hopper on the bar, dabbed pathetically at the seat, then dragged it aside (shielding his hands with napkins) so he could stand alongside Sarah. For a moment, he leaned 
\f2\i casually
\f1\i0  on the bar, then recoiled and, instead, stood 
\f2\i casually
\f1\i0  bolt-upright, hoping he might minimise infection.\
A bartender wiped down the bar and replenished complimentary bowls of peanuts and bacon. Sarah was almost certain the mixologist\'92s Mink Brown pompadour was a wig that also incorporated the sideburns.\
\'93What\'92ll it be?\'94 the bartender drawled. \'93The house special is the 
\f2\i Hunk of Burning Coal
\f1\i0 .\'94\
Sarah flipped a cocktail menu over. \'93Which one is that again?\'94\
\'93Rum, hazelnut liqueur, cr\'e8me de banana, pineapple juice, banana, and bitters. Garnished with honey and peanut butter. For an authentic experience, crumble in some bacon.\'94\
Johnny gulped hard. \'93I\'92ll just have water.\'94\
\'93We got Mountain Valley spring water, sugar,\'94 the bartender offered enthusiastically.\
\'93Is it imported?\'94\
Sarah screwed her face at Johnny\'92s stupidity.\
\'93Imported?\'94 the bartender asked. \'93Now, where would it be imported from, honey?\'94\
\'93Never mind,\'94 Sarah cut in. \'93We\'92ll both have my usual.\'94\
The bartender smiled and winked at Sarah, and headed out back.\
An arrested round of applause choked up, as a punter\'97dressed in pompadour, sun glasses and an orange-pleated jump suit\'97took the stage and grabbed the mic. She punched a number into the machine and a tune washed away the noise of the hushing crowd, a tune which, in a suspiciously high number of universes more stable than this, would be instantly recognisable as 
\f2\i The Wonder of You
\f1\i0 . In the universe of 
\f2\i this
\f1\i0  Sarah and 
\f2\i this
\f1\i0  Johnny, the songs The Elvi enjoyed attracted controversy and contention. Their titles and lyrics were a bizarre creole of mondegreens and quasi-political activist slogans. Everyone knew there must be better, truer words, but no one could agree on what they were. Needless to say, in this universe, such songs enjoyed little popularity. Except in The Grotto.\
Sarah perversely enjoyed these songs. They were earthier and somehow truer than Airborne music, which she regarded as hairdresser, style-over-substance consumer fizz. Music from 
\f2\i above
\f1\i0  the clouds was inescapably 
\f2\i in
\f1\i0  the clouds, ethereal and unemotional, and struck no chords on the strings of Sarah\'92s heart.\
The bartender returned with a pair of tall, coarse glasses, and a bottle. She popped the cork; the bottle frothed over its Airborne label.\
\'93Oh, thank God,\'94 Johnny said as the waitress poured, relieved he might escape this whole ordeal without a dose of Cloudborne dysentery.\
Sarah clinked glasses with him as the karaoke tune reached a chorus crescendo and the punter sang, \'93
\f2\i The Chunder and Spew
\f1\i0 .\'94\
As a murmur of dissent snaked through the audience, Sarah laughed, clapped with delight and bellowed at the stage, \'93I come from a land down under!\'94\
Johnny choked on his bubbles. \'93How long do you plan on torturing me with this?\'94\
\'93Oh, we\'92re here for the marathon,\'94 she said, hoping he\'92d hear the wickedness in her voice. \'93I\'92m drinking you under the bar.\'94\
\'93Tax!\'94 Johnny swore. \'93This again?\'94 He moaned with dismay, held his sweaty, bald head in his bratwurst-fingered hands, and contemplated how little health leave he had left. \'93Aren\'92t you starting your new gig in the morning?\'94\
\'93I am, and I\'92ll be fine.\'94\

\f2\i She\'92ll be pristine
\f1\i0 , Johnny conceded. Sarah could, would, and often did drink all-comers under the table. As others fell unconscious at her feet, to his knowledge, she was never the slightest shot tipsy. She never had hangovers, and never had bags under her eyes. In the years he had known her, she somehow kept herself in perfect health, never so much as a pimple. Her ever-sunny, porcelain skin and and deep-lake eyes were aphrodisiac to him, and her loved her, which was the only motivation for putting himself through this again. \
\'93Let\'92s make it fast then,\'94 he said and gulped his glass dry.
